# OpenCode Go through Perch's pinned Pi 1.0.4 harness.
# Copy this file outside the checkout and restrict it to your account before
# adding secrets. Start with Node --env-file; see README.md for the exact command.
# This example contains no credentials and is not ready to run unchanged.

# Your Go API key from https://opencode.ai/auth.
# Pi's stored provider credential takes precedence over this environment value.
OPENCODE_API_KEY=CHANGE_ME

# A separate random secret for the phone-to-bridge connection, at least 24 chars.
# This short placeholder deliberately fails the bridge's startup check.
# Do not reuse the OpenCode API key as the bridge token.
PERCH_BRIDGE_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# An existing server-side workspace in which the agent may use its enabled tools.
# Use an absolute path; Node's environment-file loader does not expand $HOME or ~.
PI_CWD=/absolute/path/to/your/agent-workspace

# Go selects subscription endpoints; "opencode" would select the Zen provider.
# Pi supplies User-Agent and conversation/session headers itself.
# The bridge adds --mode rpc; do not include --mode here.
PI_ARGS_JSON='["--provider","opencode-go","--model","glm-5.3-flash"]'

# Listen locally behind your own trusted TLS reverse proxy.
# Connect the phone to wss://your-host/session with PERCH_BRIDGE_TOKEN separately.
PERCH_BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1
PERCH_BRIDGE_PORT=8787

# Normally empty: HTTP(S) Origin matching the external Host is already accepted.
# If your proxy rewrites Host, add your exact external HTTPS origin, e.g.
# PERCH_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://perch.example.com
# Multiple additional origins are comma-separated; this is not authentication.
PERCH_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Optional separate Pi profile for its config, credentials, and session history.
# Use an absolute private path outside the checkout and preserve it on the host.
# PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=/absolute/path/to/private/pi-profile
